# Meals and food attribution

The **Meals** page is where carbs stop being numbers and start being food. It
groups your carb entries with the insulin given for them, and lets you say what
was actually on the plate.

## What a meal is

A meal event is a group of records that share a correlation identifier: the carb
entries, the boluses correlated with them, and any food attributions hanging off
those carbs. When you bolus for food — from your pump, your loop, or in Nocturne
— the insulin and the carbs are written as separate records that point at the
same event, and the Meals page reassembles them.

A carb entry with nothing correlated to it is still a meal event. It appears on
its own, with no insulin, which is exactly right for the biscuit you ate without
bolusing. Uncorrelated entries are never collapsed together, so two of them ten
minutes apart stay two events.

Each event carries its totals: total carbs, total insulin, how much of the carbs
has been attributed to specific foods, and how much has not.

## The Meals page

Meals are grouped by day, newest first, with per-day carb totals. Each row shows
the time, a label, the carbs and the insulin. The label is the food's name when
one food is attributed, a summary when several are, and otherwise the meal name
for that time of day — Breakfast, Lunch, Snack, Dinner, Late Night.

Expanding a row shows the attribution lines and the controls to change them. The
toolbar gives you a date range, sorting on any column, a search box, a filter by
food, and an **unattributed only** filter — the one you want when you sit down
to catch up on a week of unlabelled carbs.

From an expanded row you can add a food, edit a line, unlink a line, and add or
edit the insulin for the meal.

## Attributing carbs to foods

An attribution says: this carb entry included _this many portions_ of _this
food_, contributing _this many grams_. Each line holds:

| Field | Meaning |
|-------|---------|
| **Food** | The catalog entry, or nothing, for carbs you can't or won't name. |
| **Portions** | How many of the food's defined portions you ate. |
| **Carbs** | Grams this line contributes. Defaults from the food, editable. |
| **Time offset** | Minutes from the carb entry's time, for food eaten later. |
| **Note** | Free text. |

Add up the lines and you have the meal's **attributed** carbs; the rest are
**unspecified**. Both numbers are shown, and they are allowed to disagree in
either direction. You bolused for 60 g and can only account for 45 g of it —
that is a 15 g unspecified remainder, not an error, and Nocturne will not
silently rewrite either figure.

<Callout type="info" title="Attribution never changes your carbs">
  Adding, editing or removing an attribution line does not touch the carb entry's
  total. The total is what you dosed against and stays as recorded; attributions
  only describe it. If the total itself is wrong, edit the carb entry.
</Callout>

The **time offset** exists for meals eaten over an hour rather than a minute.
Pre-bolus for a restaurant meal, then attribute the bread at 0 minutes, the main
at +25, the dessert at +50, and your record reflects how the food actually
arrived.

## Matching entries from a food-logging app

If you log food in MyFitnessPal, the connector imports those entries and offers
to attach them to carb entries you already have. This saves typing the same meal
into two apps.

Each imported entry starts as **pending** and is compared against your carb
entries. An entry matches a carb entry when both are true:

- Their times are within the **match window** — 30 minutes by default.
- Their carb amounts are close enough: within **10 g**, or within **20%**.
  Either one passing is enough.

Candidates are scored on how close they are, with time weighted more heavily
than carbs, and the best one raises a notification: _Confirm you ate "porridge"
at 8:15am_, with **Accept**, **Review** and **Dismiss**.

| You choose | What happens |
|------------|--------------|
| **Accept** | An attribution line is created on that carb entry, and the imported entry is marked matched. |
| **Review** | A dialog opens so you can adjust the carbs and the time offset before accepting. |
| **Dismiss** | The imported entry is kept as a standalone food log, attached to nothing. |

Unmatched pending entries also surface directly on the Meals page as suggestion
rows next to the carb entry they might belong to, so you can work through them
without going via notifications.

The window, the tolerances and whether notifications are raised at all are
configurable per instance through the connector's matching settings.

<Callout type="tip" title="MyFitnessPal times are approximate">
  MyFitnessPal does not report a usable timestamp for a food entry, so Nocturne
  infers the time from the meal name — breakfast, lunch, dinner. That is why the
  default window is a generous 30 minutes, and why reviewing before accepting is
  worth it when the meal was late.
</Callout>

## Through the API

Meals live under `/api/v4/nutrition`:

| Endpoint | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `GET /meals` | Meal events, grouped, with totals and attributions. |
| `POST /meals` | Create a correlated bolus and carb entry in one call. |
| `GET`/`POST`/`PUT`/`DELETE` `/carbs/{id}/foods` | The attribution lines for one carb entry. |
| `/api/v4/meal-matching` | Suggestions, accept and dismiss. |

`POST /meals` is idempotent on the source's own sync identifier: post the same
meal twice from the same uploader and the second call finds the existing bolus
and reuses its correlation rather than creating a second meal.

Two _different_ apps reporting the same meal is a different problem, and is
handled separately — see
[When the same carbs arrive twice](/docs/food/deduplication).
